---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import PageJumpLinks from '../../components/PageJumpLinks.astro';
import { scenes, ocean } from '../../config/images';
import Interlude from '../../components/Interlude.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Questions to Bring', href: '/explore/questions-to-bring/' },
];
const questionAreas = [
  { label: 'Self-understanding', href: '#self-understanding', note: 'Patterns, strengths, and what keeps repeating.' },
  { label: 'Work and vocation', href: '#work-and-vocation', note: 'Conditions, direction, and real choices.' },
  { label: 'Relationships', href: '#relationships', note: 'Needs, conflict, and recurring dynamics.' },
  { label: 'Timing and transitions', href: '#timing-and-transitions', note: 'What is active now and what deserves attention.' },
  { label: 'Meaning and direction', href: '#meaning-and-direction', note: 'Development, purpose, and what asks to be lived more consciously.' },
  { label: 'Questions Mo handles carefully', href: '#questions-i-handle-carefully', note: 'Where another method or another professional belongs.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Questions to Bring to an Astrology Reading"
  description="How to bring useful questions to an astrology reading: examples for self-understanding, work, relationships, timing, and the difference between exploratory and horary questions."
  path="/explore/questions-to-bring/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Questions to bring to a reading</h1>
        <p class="lede">You do not need the perfect question. But a real question gives us somewhere useful to begin, and usually opens a better conversation than asking the chart to tell you everything at once.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.prepareReading.src}
        srcset={scenes.prepareReading.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.prepareReading.alt}
        width={scenes.prepareReading.width}
        height={scenes.prepareReading.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Build a better question in three moves</h2>
      <div class="question-builder">
        <article>
          <span class="question-builder__step">1</span>
          <h3>Name the real area</h3>
          <p>Work, a relationship, a transition, self-understanding, or direction is enough. You do not need to know which planet or house belongs to it.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="question-builder__step">2</span>
          <h3>Add what is actually happening</h3>
          <p>Move one degree more concrete: what keeps repeating, what changed, what decision is in front of you, or what no longer makes sense.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="question-builder__step">3</span>
          <h3>Leave room for discovery</h3>
          <p>Two or three genuine questions usually work better than a long checklist. The chart may show that the useful pattern sits somewhere you did not expect.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2>What makes a question useful</h2>
      <p>
        For a natal or timing consultation, I usually get more from a question when it names the
        situation you are actually living. The difference is often one degree of concreteness:
      </p>
      <ul class="question-examples">
        <li><em>“Tell me about my career”</em> → <strong>“Why does every job start well and sour in year two?”</strong></li>
        <li><em>“What about love?”</em> → <strong>“I keep choosing unavailable people. What might I be repeating?”</strong></li>
        <li><em>“Will I be successful?”</em> → <strong>“What kind of work seems to fit the way I am built?”</strong></li>
        <li><em>“Is this a good year?”</em> → <strong>“I'm weighing whether to stay or move in the spring. What does the timing add to that decision?”</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These questions give the chart something to meet in your actual life. They also leave room
        for us to discover that the most important pattern is not quite where either of us expected
        it to be.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Horary astrology is different.</strong> Horary is specifically designed around a
        clear, focused question asked at a particular moment. A question such as “Will I get this
        job?” can belong in horary in a way it does not belong in a general natal consultation.
        Different astrological methods are built to answer different kinds of questions.
      </p>

      <PageJumpLinks heading="Find examples for the question you are carrying" items={questionAreas} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Interlude
    image={ocean.ripplesSunset}
    eyebrow="Before you book"
    heading="Bring the question you are really living with."
    body="Two or three meaningful questions usually leave more room for discovery than a long list. You do not need to know which part of the chart belongs to them. That is my job."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>By area of life</h2>
      <h3 id="self-understanding">Self-understanding</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What are the patterns I keep repeating without meaning to?</li>
        <li>Which of my strengths am I underusing?</li>
        <li>Where does my chart suggest I'm hardest on myself?</li>
        <li>What's the difference between what I want and what I was taught to want?</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="work-and-vocation">Work and vocation</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What conditions does my work need: autonomy, depth, audience, structure?</li>
        <li>Is the restlessness I feel about this job, or about something larger?</li>
        <li>What does my chart suggest about working for myself versus within an organization?</li>
        <li>I've been offered a change. What should I be weighing that I'm not?</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="relationships">Relationships</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What do I actually need in a partnership, as opposed to what I look for?</li>
        <li>Why does this particular dynamic keep recurring?</li>
        <li>How do the two of us handle conflict differently, and where does that catch?</li>
        <li>What helps each of us feel secure?</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="timing-and-transitions">Timing and transitions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What season am I in, and roughly how long does it run?</li>
        <li>What periods in the next year deserve my attention around this decision?</li>
        <li>Does the timing suggest pressure to act, consolidate, reconsider, or simply pay closer attention?</li>
        <li>What's the larger arc this year belongs to?</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="meaning-and-direction">Meaning and direction</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What in my chart keeps asking to be developed?</li>
        <li>Where do my familiar strengths and my less familiar growth edges meet?</li>
        <li>What might living this chart more consciously look like in practice?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="questions-i-handle-carefully">Questions I handle carefully</h2>
      <p>
        Some questions belong to a different astrological method; others belong with a different
        professional altogether. Part of good practice is knowing the difference.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Specific outcome questions.</strong> In a natal or transit consultation, I will
          not turn a complex chart into a guaranteed yes or no. A properly framed horary question
          may be approached differently because horary has its own rules and purpose.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Medical, legal, or financial verdicts.</strong> Those belong with licensed
          professionals. Astrology may add perspective, but it does not replace their expertise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Questions about a third party's private life.</strong> I do not use someone's
          chart to investigate them without their knowledge or consent.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Death timing.</strong> I do not offer it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>“Just tell me what to do.”</strong> I understand the wish, especially when a
          decision feels heavy. My role is to help you see more of the picture, not take the choice
          away from you.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Bring two or three</h2>
      <p>
        Two or three real questions usually give us enough direction without closing off discovery.
        Write them down beforehand if that helps. And if you arrive with only one sentence, “I
        know something needs to change, but I don't know what,” we can begin there too.
      </p>
      <p>
        More in <a href="/prepare-for-your-reading/">preparing for your reading</a> and
        <a href="/how-readings-work/">what to expect</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Bring your real question"
    body="You do not have to know which reading or technique belongs to it. The Reading Finder can help narrow the options while leaving the choice with you."
    buttonLabel="Use the Reading Finder"
    buttonHref="/reading-finder/"
    secondaryLabel="See how readings work"
    secondaryHref="/how-readings-work/"
  />

  <style>
    .question-builder {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      margin-block: var(--space-6);
    }
    .question-builder article {
      position: relative;
      padding: var(--space-5);
      border: 1px solid var(--border-color, currentColor);
      border-radius: var(--radius-lg, 1rem);
    }
    .question-builder h3,
    .question-builder p { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .question-builder__step {
      display: grid;
      width: 2rem;
      height: 2rem;
      margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
      place-items: center;
      border: 1px solid currentColor;
      border-radius: 999px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .question-examples li { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
    @media (min-width: 768px) {
      .question-builder { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
